#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125768 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf x \\in V$ for some vector space $V,$ and that $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ are two bases of $V.$ \n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the **change-of-basis formula**\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that\n\n- $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\,\\mathbf x\\,]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ respectively, and\n\n- $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of vectors of the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nAs an example, suppose we are given two bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and we already know that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n![](images/d9/d9388f46f84cc0c6b1d7e5cf43ee973085366ae4f44cfe74515c59bfc1994662.svg)\n\nLet $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and ![](images/f9/f99f37621c20057077eb7c6e23690a45159cfe1b665c6b88b4a604f8687728bf.svg) Then we can easily find $[\\,\\mathbf{v}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,v\\,]_{C} & =\\overset{{P}_{B\\to C}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{[v]_{B}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "827e9581-9fde-5941-ad91-2c364796e5b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125822 :tutorial/content "We've seen that to find the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to a basis $\\mathcal{C}$ given its coordinates relative to a basis $\\mathcal B,$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [\\,x\\,]_{B}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn expanded form, the formula looks as follows:\n![](images/89/890f34433211786e2cb8aa71057de40bdf3b12678c2bbb5a79ef5e680555937f.svg)\n\nAlso, remember that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\n\t\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfffdf8e-9e9f-5304-8249-54a094cc7c11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4435 :tutorial/title "The Change-of-Basis Formula in Expanded Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125708 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Now, suppose we would like to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ \n\n- We first need to find $x_1$ and $x_2$ such that \n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+x_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_1,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/58/58010a2a5206e3fc27e3d924de70cf32dcc92c3578bdb2821ace7278fbb223cf.svg)\n\n- Similarly, we need to find $y_1$ and $y_2$ such that \n\n$$\ny_1 \\mathbf{c}_1+y_2 \\mathbf{c}_2 = \\mathbf{b}_2,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to solving the system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/68/68788aa3e388c5cba14662a947c1b7afaacb95f611c256d11a7a6d7614c352b9.svg)\n\nWe can solve both systems using Gaussian elimination. However, we can do that simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/4b/4b00a4cbd7831f6da6acd06a087d5453bd455a89eb81f6b3036b1b572bb064d1.svg)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get a matrix of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {x}_{1} & {y}_{1} \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}_{2} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the required coefficients will be stored in the columns on the right-hand side." :tutorial/id #uuid "d42ee304-2e1f-5cc2-b334-65d083ea7323" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13287 :tutorial/title "Using Gaussian Elimination to Find the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis"}]}
